# netsurf: named access -- form.elements.namedItem and the
# HTMLFormControlsCollection, RadioNodeList, form[name], document.forms,
# document[name] (patch 288)
# Generated 2026-10-13
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 287)
#
# form.elements was a new Array of the form's descendants on every read
# (querySelectorAll): no namedItem (doc 10, 283's "found, not fixed"), no
# elements.q, image buttons in it, a control outside the form
# (form="id") missing. form.q, form[0], form.length, document.forms.myform
# and document.myform were undefined -- the classic DOM older pages script
# forms with (document.myform.q.value). As in Chromium (checked case by
# case), vita_polyfill.js "named access":
#
#  - form.elements: an HTMLFormControlsCollection, the same object each
#    read, live -- the listed elements (button, fieldset, input, object,
#    output, select, textarea) whose form owner is the form, image buttons
#    not -- by index, by id or name, namedItem, item, iterable; several
#    sharing a name are a RadioNodeList (live; value: the checked radio's,
#    setting it checks the radio with that value).
#  - a form by name (its controls, else its images) and by index, and
#    form.length.
#  - document.forms: an HTMLCollection, by index and by name or id
#    (namedItem).
#  - document by name: forms, images, embeds, objects with that name, and
#    objects and named images with that id (several: a collection); an
#    iframe by name, its window.
#  - a radio button set checked by script unchecks the others of its group
#    (the same name and form owner); script-set checked was a plain
#    attribute, so a group could hold two.
#
# Named access comes from a Proxy above HTMLFormElement.prototype and
# Document.prototype: a lookup that misses every real member arrives there
# with the form or document it was made on, so the wrappers get no own
# properties (patches 278-279). Unlike browsers, a form's named controls do
# not shadow its built-in members (<input name="submit"> leaves
# form.submit the method) -- only an exotic form object could do that.
#
# Not done: a parsed iframe's contentWindow is still the frame shim (no
# document): the frame's document lives in its own content and script
# heap, and handing its window to the parent is cross-heap work of its
# own.
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-form-named.html (FORMNAMED 7/7, Chromium 7/7).
